// Part III — Reference. Declared mode: reference.
page ref_mod_http {
  sites = [:manual]
  title = "http module"

  chapter_header "http module" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-21"
    version      = "config-weave 0.1.0"
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
`use http` is an HTTP client. It is built on rustls, so it has no dependency
on the system TLS library. Every function returns `Result[…, string]` and
takes an options `Value` as its last argument. The options argument is
required: pass `Value::Null` for the defaults, or a `Value::Map` with any of
the keys below. Any other key is an `Err`. A `get` or `post` response with
an error status is still `Ok`; inspect `status`.
PROSE

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Type" | "Meaning" |
      | "`headers`" | "map of string" | "request headers" |
      | "`timeout`" | "int or float" | "seconds before the request is abandoned" |
      | "`redirects`" | "bool" | "follow redirects, default `true`" |
      | "`retries`" | "int" | "`download` only. Extra attempts after the first, default `0`." |
      | "`size`" | "int" | "`download` only. The expected byte count of the file." |
      | "`sha256`" | "string" | "`download` only. The expected SHA-256 of the file, 64 hex digits in either case." |
  }

  p "`get` and `post` reject `retries`, `size` and `sha256` as unknown options."

  h2 "HttpResponse"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
struct HttpResponse {
    status: int,
    body: string,
    headers: Map[string, string],
}
SRC
  }

  p "The result of `get` and `post`."

  h2 "get"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
http::get(url: string, opts: Value) -> Result[HttpResponse, string]
SRC
  }

  p "Sends a GET request and returns the response."

  h2 "post"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
http::post(url: string, body: string, opts: Value) -> Result[HttpResponse, string]
SRC
  }

  p "Sends `body` in a POST request and returns the response. Set the content type with a `headers` entry."

  h2 "download"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
http::download(url: string, dest: string, opts: Value) -> Result[int, string]
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
Fetches `url` and writes the body to the file at `dest`. It returns the
number of bytes written. Pair it with the [archive module](ref_mod_archive)
to unpack what was fetched.

The body is written to `<dest>.part` in the same directory. When every check
passes, the file is flushed to disk and renamed onto `dest`, replacing any
file already there. When the call fails, it removes `<dest>.part` and leaves
an existing `dest` as it was, so `dest` never holds a partial download. A
`<dest>.part` left by an earlier, interrupted call is deleted when the next
call starts.

An attempt fails in these cases:
PROSE

  table {
    rows:
      | "Failure" | "Retried" |
      | "A transport error: connect, reset, timeout, DNS." | "yes" |
      | "Status 5xx or 429." | "yes" |
      | "The body is shorter than `Content-Length` or `size`." | "yes" |
      | "Any other status that is not 2xx, such as 404." | "no" |
      | "The body is longer than `Content-Length` or `size`." | "no" |
      | "The file does not match `sha256`." | "no" |
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
With `retries` set to n, a retried failure is followed by up to n more
attempts. The pause before each is 1s, 2s, 4s and so on, capped at 30s.
When an attempt fails part way and its response carried
`Accept-Ranges: bytes`, the next attempt asks for the rest with
`Range: bytes=<n>-`, plus `If-Range` with the ETag or Last-Modified value.
The bytes already received are kept only when the server answers `206` with
a `Content-Range` starting at n. Otherwise the download starts again from
the first byte. The `size` and `sha256` checks cover the whole assembled
file.

The request asks for the identity encoding, so the bytes written are the
bytes the server stores. The error names the URL and the reason; with
`retries` set it also gives the attempt count, for example
`download https://example.org/big.iso failed after 3 attempts: body ended at
1048576 of 4194304 bytes`.
PROSE

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
use http
use value

let n = http::download("https://example.org/tool.tar.gz", "/tmp/tool.tar.gz", Value::Null)?

let opts = Value::Map(#{
    "retries": Value::Int(3),
    "sha256": Value::String("9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08"),
})
let n = http::download("https://example.org/big.iso", "/srv/media/big.iso", opts)?
SRC
  }
}
